\section*{Question 5}

Let \(f\) be a function defined on the closed interval [0,7]. The graph of \(f\), consisting of four line segments, is shown above. Let \(g\) be the function given by \(g(x)=\int_{2}^{x} f(t) d t\).
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Find \(g(3), g^{\prime}(3)\), and \(g^{\prime \prime}(3)\).
\item[(b)] Find the average rate of change of \(g\) on the interval \(0 \leq x \leq 3\).
\item[(c)] For how many values \(c\), where \(0<c<3\), is \(g^{\prime}(c)\) equal to the average rate found in part (b)? Explain your reasoning.
\item[(d)] Find the \(x\)-coordinate of each point of inflection of the graph of \(g\) on the interval \(0<x<7\). Justify your answer.
\item[(a)] \(g(3)=\int_{2}^{3} f(t) d t=\frac{1}{2}(4+2)=3\)
\[
\begin{aligned}
& g^{\prime}(3)=f(3)=2 \\
& g^{\prime \prime}(3)=f^{\prime}(3)=\frac{0-4}{4-2}=-2
\end{aligned}
\]
\item[(b)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{g(3)-g(0)}{3} & =\frac{1}{3} \int_{0}^{3} f(t) d t \\
& =\frac{1}{3}\left(\frac{1}{2}(2)(4)+\frac{1}{2}(4+2)\right)=\frac{7}{3}
\end{aligned}
\]
\item[(c)] There are two values of \(c\). We need \(\frac{7}{3}=g^{\prime}(c)=f(c)\) The graph of \(f\) intersects the line \(y=\frac{7}{3}\) at two places between 0 and 3.
\item[(d)] \(x=2\) and \(x=5\) because \(g^{\prime}=f\) changes from increasing to decreasing at \(x=2\), and from decreasing to increasing at \(x=5\).
\end{itemize}
\(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: g(3) \\ 1: g^{\prime}(3) \\ 1: g^{\prime \prime}(3)\end{array}\right.\)
\begin{itemize}
\item[] \(2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: g(3)-g(0)=\int_{0}^{3} f(t) d t \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[] \(2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: \text { answer of } 2 \\ 1: \text { reason }\end{array}\right.\)
\end{itemize}

Note: 1/2 if answer is 1 by MVT
